{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-4700" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2x({x}^{3}+1)}{3{x}^{4}-7}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-4700" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The given function can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{4}+2x}{3{x}^{4}-7}\n$$\n\nTo find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{4}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{4},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2{x}^{4}+2x}{3{x}^{4}-7} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{2x}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{7}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})}{(3-\\frac{7}{{x}^{4}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}$ and $\\frac{7}{{x}^{4}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(2+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})}{(3-\\frac{7}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{2+0}{3-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=\\frac{2}{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-4700" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f95f86778bf72240e59b5fd0f17af4f4dddafc85913d865c413e552f7c05427d"} {"question" "q-4700" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-4700" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=1"] ["math" "y=2"] ["math" "y=3"] ["math" "y=\\frac{2}{3}"] ["text" "No horizontal asymptote exist"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-4700" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=\\frac{2}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-277946" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{4{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+3}{2-{x}^{3}}$ is $y=${{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-277946" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn this case, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{3}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{4{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+3}{2-{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(4-\\frac{5}{x}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}-1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{5}{x},$ $\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}},$ and $\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(4-\\frac{5}{x}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}-1)} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{4-0+0}{0-1} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-277946" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f95f86778bf72240e59b5fd0f17af4f4dddafc85913d865c413e552f7c05427d"} {"question" "q-277946" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-277946" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-277950" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{8{x}^{4}+6x}{({x}^{2}-2x)(1-2{x}^{2})}$ is $y=${{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-277950" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The given function can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{8{x}^{4}-6x}{-2{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-2x}\n$$\n\nTo find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{4}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{4},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{8{x}^{4}-6x}{-2{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{8{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{6x}{{x}^{4}})}{(-\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{2x}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(8-\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}})}{(-2+\\frac{4}{x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}},$ $\\frac{4}{x},$ $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},$ and $\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(8-\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}})}{(-2+\\frac{4}{x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{8-0}{-2+0+0-0} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-277950" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f95f86778bf72240e59b5fd0f17af4f4dddafc85913d865c413e552f7c05427d"} {"question" "q-277950" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-277950" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-277944" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-8{x}^{3}}{2{x}^{3}-5x}$ is $y=${{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-277944" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn this case, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{3}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-8{x}^{3}}{2{x}^{3}-5x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{8{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{5x}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x}-8)}{(2-\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{1}{x}$ and $\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x}-8)}{(2-\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0-8}{2+0} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-277944" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f95f86778bf72240e59b5fd0f17af4f4dddafc85913d865c413e552f7c05427d"} {"question" "q-277944" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-277944" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-6931" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{4{x}^{2}+6x+1}{1-2{x}^{2}}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-6931" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn this case, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{2}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{4{x}^{2}+6x+1}{1-2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{6x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(4+\\frac{6}{x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{6}{x}$ and $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(4+\\frac{6}{x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-2)} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{4+0+0}{0-2} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is  $y=-2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-6931" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f95f86778bf72240e59b5fd0f17af4f4dddafc85913d865c413e552f7c05427d"} {"question" "q-6931" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-6931" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=-2"] ["math" "y=-3"] ["math" "y=1"] ["math" "y=3"] ["text" "No horizontal asymptotes"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-6931" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=-2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-4637" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{3x-{x}^{2}}{5{x}^{4}-4}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-4637" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{4}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{4},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3x-{x}^{2}}{5{x}^{4}-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3x}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{5{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{4}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(5-\\frac{4}{{x}^{4}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}},\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ and $\\frac{4}{{x}^{4}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(5-\\frac{4}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0-0}{5-0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-4637" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f95f86778bf72240e59b5fd0f17af4f4dddafc85913d865c413e552f7c05427d"} {"question" "q-4637" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-4637" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=-\\frac{3}{5}"] ["math" "y=0"] ["math" "y=\\frac{3}{5}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{4}{5}"] ["text" "No horizontal asymptotes"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-4637" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-4699" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+2x-3}{{x}^{3}-x}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-4699" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{3}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+2x-3}{{x}^{3}-x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}})}{(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{1}{x},\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}},\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}$ and $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}})}{(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0+0-0}{1-0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-4699" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f95f86778bf72240e59b5fd0f17af4f4dddafc85913d865c413e552f7c05427d"} {"question" "q-4699" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-4699" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=-2"] ["math" "y=0"] ["math" "y=2"] ["math" "y=\\frac{1}{3}"] ["text" "No horizontal asymptotes"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-4699" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-6998" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{8-3{x}^{3}}{1-16{x}^{4}}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-6998" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{4}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{4},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{8-3{x}^{3}}{1-16{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{8}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{16{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{8}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{3}{x})}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-16)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{8}{{x}^{4}},\\frac{3}{x}$ and  $\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}$  become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{8}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{3}{x})}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-16)} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0-0}{0-16} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-6998" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f95f86778bf72240e59b5fd0f17af4f4dddafc85913d865c413e552f7c05427d"} {"question" "q-6998" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-6998" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "y=-\\frac{3}{16}"] ["math" "y=0"] ["math" "y=8"] ["text" "No horizontal asymptotes"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-6998" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-269988" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{7{x}^{3}+2x-5}{{x}^{2}(2{x}^{2}+3)}$ is $y=${{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-269988" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The given function can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{7{x}^{3}+2x-5}{2{x}^{4}+3{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nTo find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{4}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{4},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{7{x}^{3}+2x-5}{2{x}^{4}+3{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{7{x}^{3}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{2x}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{5}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{7}{x}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{5}{{x}^{4}})}{(2+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{7}{x},$ $\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}},$ $\\frac{5}{{x}^{4}},$ and $\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{7}{x}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{5}{{x}^{4}})}{(2+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0+0-0}{2+0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-269988" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f95f86778bf72240e59b5fd0f17af4f4dddafc85913d865c413e552f7c05427d"} {"question" "q-269988" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-269988" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-7006" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2x+1}{9x+8{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-7006" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{3}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2x+1}{9x+8{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{9x}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{8{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{8}{x}-1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}},\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}},\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}}$ and $\\frac{8}{x}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{8}{x}-1)} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0+0}{0+0-1} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-7006" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f95f86778bf72240e59b5fd0f17af4f4dddafc85913d865c413e552f7c05427d"} {"question" "q-7006" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-7006" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=-2"] ["math" "y=-\\frac{1}{9}"] ["math" "y=0"] ["math" "y=1"] ["math" "y=\\frac{2}{9}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-7006" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-81202" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{3+4{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}-x}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-81202" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{2}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3+4{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}-x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}+4-2x)}{(1-\\frac{1}{x})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}$ and $\\frac{1}{x}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}+4-2x)}{(1-\\frac{1}{x})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{0+4-2x}{1-0} \\\\  & =4-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y=f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-81202" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f95f86778bf72240e59b5fd0f17af4f4dddafc85913d865c413e552f7c05427d"} {"question" "q-81202" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-81202" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=-2"] ["math" "y=-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "y=3"] ["math" "y=4"] ["text" "No horizontal asymptotes"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-81202" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "No horizontal asymptotes"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-7005" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{6-3x+4{x}^{2}}{12x-21}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-7005" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is $x.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{6-3x+4{x}^{2}}{12x-21} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{6}{x}-\\frac{3x}{x}+\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{x})}{(\\frac{12x}{x}-\\frac{21}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{6}{x}-3+4x)}{(12-\\frac{21}{x})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{6}{x}$ and $\\frac{21}{x}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{6}{x}-3+4x)}{(12-\\frac{21}{x})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{0-3+4x}{12-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{4x-3}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y=f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-7005" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f95f86778bf72240e59b5fd0f17af4f4dddafc85913d865c413e552f7c05427d"} {"question" "q-7005" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-7005" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=0"] ["math" "y=\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{7}{4}"] ["text" "No horizontal asymptotes"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-7005" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "No horizontal asymptotes"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-6993" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}+3}{4{x}^{2}-6x}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-6993" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{2}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{3}+3}{4{x}^{2}-6x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{6x}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})}{(4-\\frac{6}{x})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}$ and $\\frac{6}{x}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(x+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})}{(4-\\frac{6}{x})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{x+0}{4-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y=f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-6993" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f95f86778bf72240e59b5fd0f17af4f4dddafc85913d865c413e552f7c05427d"} {"question" "q-6993" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-6993" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=-1"] ["math" "y=-\\frac{3}{4}"] ["math" "y=0"] ["math" "y=\\frac{1}{4}"] ["text" "No horizontal asymptotes"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-6993" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "No horizontal asymptotes"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-113178" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{4}-3x+2}{{x}^{2}+1}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-113178" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{2}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{4}-3x+2}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{3x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{x}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})}{(1+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{3}{x},$ $\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}$ and $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{({x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{x}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})}{(1+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{{x}^{2}-0+0}{1+0} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y=f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-113178" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f95f86778bf72240e59b5fd0f17af4f4dddafc85913d865c413e552f7c05427d"} {"question" "q-113178" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-113178" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=-3"] ["math" "y=0"] ["math" "y=1"] ["math" "y=2"] ["text" "No horizontal asymptotes"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-113178" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "No horizontal asymptotes"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-4003" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}+2x+1}{{x}^{2}-x-12}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-4003" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{2}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{3}+2x+1}{{x}^{2}-x-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(1-\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{2}{x},\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},\\frac{1}{x}$ and $\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(x+\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(1-\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{x+0+0}{1-0-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{1} \\\\  & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y=f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-4003" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f95f86778bf72240e59b5fd0f17af4f4dddafc85913d865c413e552f7c05427d"} {"question" "q-4003" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-4003" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=-3"] ["math" "y=-\\frac{1}{12}"] ["math" "y=0"] ["math" "y=4"] ["text" "No horizontal asymptotes"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "q-4003" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "No horizontal asymptotes"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa6d73f8a7e134b082aa10094a0e1996a79def241d1eeed15a31d95d322d9286"} {"question" "e-5847" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{4x^3-x}{3x^3+24}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/example-5847.json" "file_sha256" "18094162e3487476287f26702b937bb67c6e448dda5ea9ff87b8c47bfe91a101"} {"question" "e-5847" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to\\infty.$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is $x^3.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by $x^3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{4{x}^{3}-x}{3{x}^{3}+24} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{24}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(4-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(3+\\frac{24}{{x}^{3}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$ and $\\dfrac{24}{x^3}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(4-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(3+\\frac{24}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{4-0}{3+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y = \\dfrac{4}{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/example-5847.json" "file_sha256" "18094162e3487476287f26702b937bb67c6e448dda5ea9ff87b8c47bfe91a101"} {"question" "e-5849" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{3x^2+ 2x}{6x^4-1}$." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/example-5849.json" "file_sha256" "cfed3bae0bdbdd756a458930381e122e572106a5f47969d7455fcff76eda0a77"} {"question" "e-5849" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to\\infty.$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is $x^4.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by $x^4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}+2x}{6{x}^{4}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{2x}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{6{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})}{(6-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms  $\\dfrac{3}{x^2}, \\dfrac{2}{x^3},$ and $\\dfrac{1}{x^4}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})}{(6-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0+0}{6-0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y = 0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/example-5849.json" "file_sha256" "cfed3bae0bdbdd756a458930381e122e572106a5f47969d7455fcff76eda0a77"} {"question" "e-5851" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the horizontal asymptote of $f(x) = \\dfrac{2x^3}{x^2-1}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/example-5851.json" "file_sha256" "dd80250c95c321f4c7b3a4733ee65ab64dd1d5f8aad6e2da5f209c3bc35e1986"} {"question" "e-5851" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to\\infty.$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is $x^2.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by $x^2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the term $\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$ becomes very small. Therefore, as $x\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2x}{(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{2x}{1-0} \\\\  & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y = f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060472/example-5851.json" "file_sha256" "dd80250c95c321f4c7b3a4733ee65ab64dd1d5f8aad6e2da5f209c3bc35e1986"}] "usage" {"q-4700" [] "e-5851" [] "e-5847" [] "q-6993" [] "e-5849" [] "q-7006" []} "basis" 1934 "retractions" [[17592186088130 :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x) = (2x(x^{3} + 1))/(3x^{4} - 7)$."] [17592186088224 :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x) = \\frac{2x + 1}{9x + 8x^{2} - x^{3}}$."] [17592186088221 :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of $f(x) = \\frac{x^{3} + 3}{4x^{2} - 6x}$."] [17592186087513 :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{4x^3-x}{3x^3+24}$."] [17592186087513 :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to\\infty$.\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is $x^3$. So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by $x^3$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{4x^{3} - x}{3x^{3} + 24} \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{4x^{3}}{x^{3}} - \\frac{x}{x^{3}}))/((\\frac{3x^{3}}{x^{3}} + \\frac{24}{x^{3}})) \\\\\n&= ((4 - \\frac{1}{x^{2}}))/((3 + \\frac{24}{x^{3}}))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{x^2}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{24}{x^3}\n$$\n\nbecome very small. Therefore, as $x\\to\\infty$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= ((4 - \\frac{1}{x^{2}}))/((3 + \\frac{24}{x^{3}})) \\\\\n&= → \\frac{4 - 0}{3 + 0} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{4}{3}\n$$"] [17592186087514 :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to\\infty$.\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is $x^4$. So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by $x^4$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{3x^{2} + 2x}{6x^{4} - 1} \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{3x^{2}}{x^{4}} + \\frac{2x}{x^{4}}))/((\\frac{6x^{4}}{x^{4}} - \\frac{1}{x^{4}})) \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{3}{x^{2}} + \\frac{2}{x^{3}}))/((6 - \\frac{1}{x^{4}}))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{x^2}, \\dfrac{2}{x^3}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{x^4}\n$$\n\nbecome very small. Therefore, as $x\\to\\infty$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= ((\\frac{3}{x^{2}} + \\frac{2}{x^{3}}))/((6 - \\frac{1}{x^{4}})) \\\\\n&= → \\frac{0 + 0}{6 - 0} \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y = 0$."] [17592186087515 :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of $f(x) = \\dfrac{2x^3}{x^2-1}$."] [17592186087515 :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to\\infty$.\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is $x^2$. So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by $x^2$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{2x^{3}}{x^{2} - 1} \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{2x^{3}}{x^{2}}))/((\\frac{x^{2}}{x^{2}} - \\frac{1}{x^{2}})) \\\\\n&= (2x)/((1 - \\frac{1}{x^{2}}))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the term\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{x^2}\n$$\n\nbecomes very small. Therefore, as $x\\to\\infty$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= (2x)/((1 - \\frac{1}{x^{2}})) \\\\\n&\\approx \\frac{2x}{1 - 0} \\\\\n&= 2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y = f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186085712 17592186085713 17592186085714 17592186085715 17592186085716 17592186086258 17592186086259 17592186086260 17592186086261 17592186086262 17592186086276 17592186086277 17592186086278 17592186086279 17592186086280] "immutable_fields" [[17592186085711 :answer-field/correct] [17592186085711 :answer-field/id] [17592186085711 :answer-field/key] [17592186085711 :answer-field/type] [17592186086257 :answer-field/correct] [17592186086257 :answer-field/id] [17592186086257 :answer-field/key] [17592186086257 :answer-field/type] [17592186086275 :answer-field/correct] [17592186086275 :answer-field/id] [17592186086275 :answer-field/key] [17592186086275 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
